bracket_validator
=================

Write a function:

    def bracket_validator(s: str) -> bool

Given a string `s` that may contain the characters '(', ')', '{', '}',
'[' and ']' (possibly mixed with other characters), return True if every
opening bracket is correctly closed by the matching closing bracket in
the right order, and False otherwise.

Rules:
  - Every opener must have a matching closer of the same type.
  - Brackets must close in the reverse order they were opened
    (so "([)]" is INVALID).
  - An empty string is considered valid.
  - Non-bracket characters are ignored.

Examples:
    bracket_validator("()[]{}")   -> True
    bracket_validator("([{}])")   -> True
    bracket_validator("(]")       -> False
    bracket_validator("([)]")     -> False
    bracket_validator("")         -> True

# ---

# List Methods / Operations (`stack`)

# `stack` is a list.

# | Method / Operation   | Purpose                              |
# | -------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
# | `stack.append(char)` | Adds an opening bracket to the list  |
# | `stack.pop()`        | Removes the last opening bracket     |
# | `stack[-1]`          | Gets the last item in the list       |
# | `not stack`          | Checks if the list is empty          |
# | `len(stack)`         | Gets the number of items in the list |

# ---

# # Dictionary Methods / Operations (`pairs`)

# `pairs` is a dictionary.

# | Method / Operation | Purpose                                                 |
# | ------------------ | ------------------------------------------------------- |
# | `pairs.values()`   | Gets all opening brackets                               |
# | `pairs.keys()`     | Gets all closing brackets                               |
# | `pairs[char]`      | Gets the matching opening bracket for a closing bracket |
